{
  "id": "u08-l01",
  "unit": "u08-daily-routine",
  "title": "I do / you do",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali verbs change their ending for the person, and the endings are very regular. Here is **করা** (*kora*, 'to do'):\n\n- আমি / আমরা — **করি** (*kori*) 'I / we do'\n- তুই — **করিস** (*korish*) 'you do' (intimate)\n- তুমি / তোমরা — **করো** (*koro*) 'you do' (familiar)\n- আপনি / তিনি — **করেন** (*koren*) 'you do' (polite), 'he/she does' (polite)\n- সে / তারা — **করে** (*kore*) 'he/she does', 'they do'\n\nThe verb doesn't change for plural: আমি **করি**, আমরা **করি**; সে **করে**, তারা **করে**. Polite 'you' and polite 'he/she' share **-েন** — so you say **আমার বাবা কাজ করেন** with the same ending as **আপনি**.\n\nMany everyday actions are **noun + করা**: **কাজ করা** 'to work' (lit. 'do work'). **আপনি কী করেন?** 'What do you do?' is how people ask about your job.\n\nFor 'don't', put **না** after the verb: **আমি কাজ করি না** 'I don't work'.\n\nUseful reactions: **সত্যি?** 'Really?' and **আসলে…** 'Actually…'.",
